In article <32382@netnews.upenn.edu>, kehoe@scotty.dccs.upenn.edu (Brendan Kehoe) writes...
>In <1990Nov6.104442.7340@umiami.ir.miami.edu>, gv9b2c9z@umiami.ir.miami.edu writes:
>>Has anyone successfully gotten NetHack 3.0 to run on a Vax with VMS?
>
> I have .. it went really smoothly. The only problem was making it
>run. You have to copy the [.OTHERS]TERMCAP. file to wherever your
>playground is, then create the file NETHACK.COM in that directory:
> 
>	$ DEFINE/USER TERMCAP THIS_DISK:[THIS.DIR]TERMCAP.
>	$ RUN THIS_DISK:[THIS.DIR]NETHACK
> 
>and have people invoke nethack with @THIS_DISK:[THIS.DIR]NETHACK so
>that the TERMCAP environmental variable exists. Otherwise it will
>never recognize your terminal.

     If you're going to go this route (which is OK in general) you need
some revisions:
|$ this_file = f$environ("PROCEDURE")	!full name of this .COM file
|$ this_dir  = f$parse(this_file,,,"DEVICE") + f$parse(this_file,,,"DIRECTORY")
|$ define/user termcap 'this_dir'termcap
|$ define/user sys$input sys$command:	!read from terminal instead of .com file
|$ mcr 'this_dir'nethack "''p1'" "''p2'" "''p3'" "''p4'" "''p5'" "''p6'"

This passes command line parameters to NetHack, so that you can specify
a player name and/or character type, explore mode switch, and so on without
changing your NETHACKOPTIONS.
|$ nethack :== @<wherever>nethack.com	!stick this in login.com
|$ nethack -u "Conan-B" -x "-DEC"	!start a game in explore mode, using
					!+  vt100 line-drawing graphics

> If anybody has a better way of doing this (or if I overlooked
>something in my building it), please speak up.

     If you don't use TERMCAP for anything else on your system--which you
must not or else you wouldn't need the one from [.others]--and if you have
the cooperation of your system manager (always advisable :-), you can
arrange to have TERMCAP defined system-wide at boot time.  That way you
don't have to worry about it when starting up the game.  As another
alternative, stick
|$ define/job termcap <wherever>termcap !substitute real location for <wherever>
|$ nethack :== $<wherever>nethack	!note '$' prefix
in your login.com.

      If you're not using a Digital terminal or clone, you may have to
add a new entry to the termcap file, and also define NETHACK_TERM as the
type of terminal.
|$ define nethack_term "adm3a"	!yecch ;-}
     The same applies for using at vt420 under VMS v5.4; the C library
didn't keep up with the rest of VMS and still doesn't know about this
fairly new terminal.
|$ define nethack_term "vt300"	!or "vt320"

     Note:  the various references to <wherever> should actually be
"HACKDIR:", which should be defined to point at the appropriate directory,
preferrably system-wide ala TERMCAP.

